WEBVTT

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:04.329
Welcome to Day 1 of Case Management Training with Legal Support Help.

00:00:04.679 --> 00:00:09.439
Over the next session, we will build the foundation for everything that follows this

00:00:09.439 --> 00:00:14.085
week: what a Case Manager actually owns, how a case moves from its very first call

00:00:14.085 --> 00:00:17.598
to its final resolution, and how you keep that movement clean,

00:00:17.598 --> 00:00:18.902
compliant, and on time.

00:00:19.252 --> 00:00:20.587
Take notes as we go.

00:00:20.937 --> 00:00:24.327
Every idea on these slides comes back in your practice work.

00:00:26.877 --> 00:00:28.804
Here is our agenda for today.

00:00:29.154 --> 00:00:32.823
Part one: we understand the fundamentals of case management.

00:00:33.173 --> 00:00:36.175
Part two: we work through intake, case evaluation,

00:00:36.175 --> 00:00:38.757
and the decision to approve representation.

00:00:39.107 --> 00:00:44.047
Part three: we move into the Treatment Phase, where most of a personal injury case's

00:00:44.047 --> 00:00:45.400
value is built or lost.

00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:50.305
Each part ends with a skill-building exercise, so you will apply what you learn

00:00:50.305 --> 00:00:50.939
right away.

00:00:53.489 --> 00:00:55.161
So, what is case management?

00:00:55.511 --> 00:00:59.339
It is the end-to-end coordination, tracking, and handling of a case,

00:00:59.339 --> 00:01:01.479
from intake all the way to resolution.

00:01:01.829 --> 00:01:05.463
Think of it as three tests that every task on a file must pass.

00:01:05.813 --> 00:01:08.869
First, timely progression: the case keeps moving,

00:01:08.869 --> 00:01:11.176
and nothing sits without a next step.

00:01:11.526 --> 00:01:16.691
Second, proper documentation and compliance: everything is recorded and done by the

00:01:16.691 --> 00:01:17.064
rules.

00:01:17.414 --> 00:01:20.371
Third, effective communication between all parties:

00:01:20.371 --> 00:01:25.240
the client, the attorney, the carriers, and the providers all know what they need to

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:25.530
know.

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:30.831
This is the general process, in seven stages.

00:01:31.181 --> 00:01:33.352
One: intake and case opening.

00:01:33.702 --> 00:01:36.291
Two: case planning and strategy support.

00:01:36.641 --> 00:01:39.497
Three: documentation and file management.

00:01:39.847 --> 00:01:41.867
Four: communication management.

00:01:42.217 --> 00:01:44.539
Five: case monitoring and tracking.

00:01:44.889 --> 00:01:47.571
Six: billing and documentation support.

00:01:47.921 --> 00:01:50.533
And seven: case resolution and closing.

00:01:50.883 --> 00:01:52.033
Notice the arrows.

00:01:52.383 --> 00:01:56.781
The case moves through every stage in order, and the Case Manager carries the file

00:01:56.781 --> 00:01:57.747
through all seven.

00:01:58.097 --> 00:02:00.349
You are the one constant from start to finish.

00:02:02.899 --> 00:02:05.290
These are a Case Manager's key duties.

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:07.498
Case updates and summaries.

00:02:07.848 --> 00:02:10.878
Research on relevant laws and legal compliance.

00:02:11.228 --> 00:02:13.446
Legal document drafting and filing.

00:02:13.796 --> 00:02:15.560
Task compliance monitoring.

00:02:15.910 --> 00:02:17.327
Client communication.

00:02:17.677 --> 00:02:21.206
Case health analysis, which means reading the whole file for risk.

00:02:21.556 --> 00:02:23.123
Case progress monitoring.

00:02:23.473 --> 00:02:24.820
Resource allocation.

00:02:25.170 --> 00:02:27.156
And planning and recommendation.

00:02:27.505 --> 00:02:30.345
Notice the last one: you plan and you recommend,

00:02:30.345 --> 00:02:33.717
while decisions of legal strategy stay with the attorney.

00:02:36.267 --> 00:02:40.768
Case management is divided into structured phases that guide how a case is handled

00:02:40.768 --> 00:02:41.921
from start to finish.

00:02:42.271 --> 00:02:45.007
Each phase has its own goals, responsibilities,

00:02:45.007 --> 00:02:49.490
and deliverables, and together they keep the case progressing efficiently and

00:02:49.490 --> 00:02:50.131
accurately.

00:02:50.481 --> 00:02:54.779
The phases are: intake; investigation, also called claim set up;

00:02:54.779 --> 00:02:59.078
treatment; pre-demand; demand; settlement and lien negotiations;

00:02:59.078 --> 00:03:02.234
disbursement; and, when a case does not settle,

00:03:02.234 --> 00:03:02.973
litigation.

00:03:03.323 --> 00:03:05.204
Today we focus on the first three.

00:03:07.754 --> 00:03:11.191
The Intake Phase is where Case Managers establish control,

00:03:11.191 --> 00:03:14.569
structure, and clarity from the very beginning of a case.

00:03:14.919 --> 00:03:16.695
This is not just data collection.

00:03:17.045 --> 00:03:19.909
It is your first opportunity to ensure accuracy,

00:03:19.909 --> 00:03:22.177
compliance, and proper case direction.

00:03:22.527 --> 00:03:26.090
Your role is to verify and organize essential case information,

00:03:26.090 --> 00:03:29.144
while making sure the case meets acceptance standards.

00:03:29.494 --> 00:03:34.271
Intake is responsible for four things: capturing complete and accurate client

00:03:34.271 --> 00:03:38.861
details, screening for conflicts of interest, assessing case viability and

00:03:38.861 --> 00:03:42.707
eligibility, and establishing a properly structured case file.

00:03:43.057 --> 00:03:46.667
This phase decides whether the case moves forward or is declined.

00:03:49.217 --> 00:03:52.131
Here is what intake looks like in a real workflow.

00:03:52.481 --> 00:03:55.419
You speak with the client, or review what they submitted.

00:03:55.769 --> 00:04:00.970
You ask structured questions: who, what, when, where, and how.

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:04.849
You run a conflict check in the system before anything else moves.

00:04:05.199 --> 00:04:08.067
You review documents for completeness and relevance.

00:04:08.417 --> 00:04:12.887
And you create the case file with proper naming conventions and a system entry.

00:04:13.237 --> 00:04:17.481
The example workflow runs like this: the client submits an inquiry form;

00:04:17.481 --> 00:04:21.430
you review and verify the details; the conflict check is completed;

00:04:21.430 --> 00:04:26.205
the case is approved or escalated for review; and the file is created and logged.

00:04:26.555 --> 00:04:31.163
Working independently, you should be able to run that whole sequence and communicate

00:04:31.163 --> 00:04:34.125
next steps clearly to the client or the internal team.

00:04:36.675 --> 00:04:40.862
Initial client contact is the first interaction with a potential client,

00:04:40.862 --> 00:04:42.665
followed immediately by intake.

00:04:43.015 --> 00:04:45.920
It is used to gather basic facts, assess urgency,

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:48.588
and decide whether the case can move forward.

00:04:48.938 --> 00:04:52.375
The Case Manager's role begins after intake is completed.

00:04:52.725 --> 00:04:57.314
You act as the quality-control and case-validation reviewer before a case is

00:04:57.314 --> 00:05:00.213
officially accepted into active case management.

00:05:00.563 --> 00:05:04.685
In other words, Case Managers are the gatekeepers of case quality.

00:05:05.035 --> 00:05:07.869
Your job is to make sure intake work is complete,

00:05:07.869 --> 00:05:11.397
accurate, properly documented, and ready for case activation.

00:05:13.947 --> 00:05:18.055
After you complete the verification call and confirm the facts of loss,

00:05:18.055 --> 00:05:21.354
the case moves into review and evaluation for acceptance.

00:05:21.704 --> 00:05:24.769
At this point, you are no longer gathering information.

00:05:25.119 --> 00:05:28.433
You are assessing whether the firm will accept the case,

00:05:28.433 --> 00:05:31.156
based on verified facts and a complete intake.

00:05:31.506 --> 00:05:34.500
This step makes sure the case is factually confirmed,

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:38.681
properly documented, and aligned with firm criteria before it is accepted.

00:05:41.231 --> 00:05:43.843
Now let's talk about where intake goes wrong.

00:05:44.193 --> 00:05:48.210
Intake bottlenecks are points in the process where work slows down,

00:05:48.210 --> 00:05:51.507
gets stuck, or becomes inconsistent, delaying the case.

00:05:51.857 --> 00:05:56.240
They usually come from five sources: missing or incomplete information,

00:05:56.240 --> 00:06:00.128
delayed client responses, poorly documented intake submissions,

00:06:00.128 --> 00:06:04.942
verification or conflict check delays, and unclear case eligibility decisions.

00:06:05.292 --> 00:06:08.227
Over the next few slides, we will look at each one,

00:06:08.227 --> 00:06:10.760
and at what it looks like on an actual file.

00:06:13.310 --> 00:06:16.364
The first bottleneck is incomplete client information.

00:06:16.714 --> 00:06:19.732
Watch for missing contact details or incident facts,

00:06:19.732 --> 00:06:22.809
intake packets with no supporting documents attached,

00:06:22.809 --> 00:06:24.899
and vague or unclear case summaries.

00:06:25.249 --> 00:06:28.878
Every one of these sends you back to the client before the case can move,

00:06:28.878 --> 00:06:31.413
so catch them on your first review, not your third.

00:06:33.963 --> 00:06:36.657
The second bottleneck is delayed client follow-up.

00:06:37.007 --> 00:06:39.979
Clients may not respond to clarification requests.

00:06:40.329 --> 00:06:42.999
Verification call confirmations may be missing.

00:06:43.349 --> 00:06:45.567
And required documents may come in slowly.

00:06:45.917 --> 00:06:49.877
Each delay pushes the whole timeline back, so follow up promptly,

00:06:49.877 --> 00:06:52.070
and document every attempt you make.

00:06:54.620 --> 00:06:57.035
The third bottleneck is the conflict check.

00:06:57.385 --> 00:07:00.967
Delays here come from system backlogs or manual verification,

00:07:00.967 --> 00:07:05.548
from incomplete conflict check entries, and from unclear information about the

00:07:05.548 --> 00:07:06.429
opposing party.

00:07:06.779 --> 00:07:10.134
Remember, the conflict check comes before anything else moves.

00:07:10.484 --> 00:07:14.362
A conflict check run on half the information is not a conflict check.

00:07:16.912 --> 00:07:21.264
The fourth bottleneck is intake form errors: incorrect dates or inconsistent

00:07:21.264 --> 00:07:24.357
statements, missing signatures or authorization forms,

00:07:24.357 --> 00:07:26.304
and duplicate or misfiled records.

00:07:26.654 --> 00:07:29.754
These errors can follow a case all the way to the demand,

00:07:29.754 --> 00:07:33.017
so correct them at intake, while they are still easy to fix.

00:07:35.567 --> 00:07:38.782
The fifth bottleneck is case eligibility uncertainty.

00:07:39.132 --> 00:07:42.232
An intake may be submitted without a clear legal assessment.

00:07:42.582 --> 00:07:46.181
The case may need attorney review before a decision can be made.

00:07:46.531 --> 00:07:50.432
Or the facts of loss, or of liability, may be ambiguous.

00:07:50.782 --> 00:07:53.557
When eligibility is unclear, don't guess.

00:07:53.907 --> 00:07:57.506
Escalate the file for attorney review, and document why.

00:08:00.056 --> 00:08:04.519
Inside a legal framework, case management shifts from best efforts to strict

00:08:04.519 --> 00:08:05.164
compliance.

00:08:05.514 --> 00:08:09.097
Your tools make that possible: case management software,

00:08:09.097 --> 00:08:12.744
task trackers and calendars, document management systems,

00:08:12.744 --> 00:08:14.663
and workflow automation tools.

00:08:15.013 --> 00:08:17.625
System equals efficiency plus accountability.

00:08:17.975 --> 00:08:21.861
And here is the strategy behind the personal injury case lifecycle:

00:08:21.861 --> 00:08:25.108
every case is a race against the Statute of Limitations,

00:08:25.108 --> 00:08:29.167
and against the gap in treatment defense that insurance companies use.

00:08:29.517 --> 00:08:33.674
In intake and investigation, the focus is liability:

00:08:33.674 --> 00:08:37.590
confirm insurance coverage, including PIP/MedPay,

00:08:37.590 --> 00:08:43.026
bodily injury, and UM/UIM; order police reports and 911 transcripts;

00:08:43.026 --> 00:08:46.143
and identify every potential defendant.

00:08:46.493 --> 00:08:50.284
In treatment, the focus is damages: track client compliance,

00:08:50.284 --> 00:08:55.529
make sure there are no gaps in treatment, and gather diagnostic imaging like MRI/CT

00:08:55.529 --> 00:08:58.625
results right away, to make the injury objective.

00:08:58.975 --> 00:09:01.995
In the demand phase, the focus is value maximization:

00:09:01.995 --> 00:09:05.756
compile a special damages summary of medical bills and lost wages,

00:09:05.756 --> 00:09:10.144
and request final narratives from surgeons to establish permanent impairment.

00:09:10.494 --> 00:09:13.818
And in litigation, the focus is evidence preservation:

00:09:13.818 --> 00:09:17.141
manage the discovery calendar, coordinate depositions,

00:09:17.141 --> 00:09:19.480
and facilitate expert witness reviews.

00:09:22.030 --> 00:09:24.410
The last bottleneck is communication gaps.

00:09:24.760 --> 00:09:27.732
The intake team may not be updating Case Managers.

00:09:28.082 --> 00:09:31.530
A Case Manager may not receive the complete intake packet.

00:09:31.880 --> 00:09:34.167
Or departments may simply miscommunicate.

00:09:34.517 --> 00:09:38.561
Closing these gaps is part of your job: confirm you have the full packet,

00:09:38.561 --> 00:09:39.835
and log every hand-off.

00:09:42.385 --> 00:09:46.599
Time for your first skill-building exercise: the Intake Decision Challenge.

00:09:46.949 --> 00:09:51.977
A new potential client, John Doe, submits an inquiry about a case against Apex

00:09:51.977 --> 00:09:53.137
Delivery Services.

00:09:53.487 --> 00:09:58.180
Your task is to complete the intake evaluation and decide whether the case should be

00:09:58.180 --> 00:10:00.024
accepted, escalated, or declined.

00:10:00.374 --> 00:10:02.905
Accepted means it moves to case opening.

00:10:03.255 --> 00:10:06.354
Escalated means it goes to an attorney or senior reviewer.

00:10:06.704 --> 00:10:10.245
Declined means it fails on eligibility or risk factors.

00:10:10.595 --> 00:10:14.304
Whichever you choose, clearly document the reason for your decision,

00:10:14.304 --> 00:10:17.794
and communicate the next steps to the intake team or the client.

00:10:20.344 --> 00:10:22.700
Now apply what you learned about bottlenecks.

00:10:23.050 --> 00:10:25.978
Identify where intake is breaking down on the file,

00:10:25.978 --> 00:10:28.159
and what actions will move it forward.

00:10:28.509 --> 00:10:29.902
Work through four steps.

00:10:30.252 --> 00:10:33.480
First, identify the areas where intake has slowed.

00:10:33.829 --> 00:10:38.218
Second, conduct a root cause analysis: why did it slow down?

00:10:38.568 --> 00:10:41.923
Third, implement immediate actions to get the file moving.

00:10:42.273 --> 00:10:45.617
And fourth, put a fix in place to prevent it from happening again.

00:10:48.167 --> 00:10:52.230
Next, we move into claim set up, the early evaluation phase.

00:10:52.580 --> 00:10:54.310
It starts with two questions.

00:10:54.660 --> 00:10:59.606
Coverage determination is the process of identifying which insurance policies apply

00:10:59.606 --> 00:11:03.182
to a claim, and what benefits, limits, and exclusions exist.

00:11:03.532 --> 00:11:06.202
Ask: is there valid insurance coverage?

00:11:06.552 --> 00:11:08.363
What type of coverage applies?

00:11:08.713 --> 00:11:10.281
What are the policy limits?

00:11:10.631 --> 00:11:13.011
And are there exclusions or coverage gaps?

00:11:13.361 --> 00:11:17.512
Liability assessment determines who is legally responsible for the incident,

00:11:17.512 --> 00:11:18.550
and to what degree.

00:11:18.900 --> 00:11:20.862
Ask: who caused the incident?

00:11:21.212 --> 00:11:24.301
Is liability clear, shared, or disputed?

00:11:24.651 --> 00:11:26.206
What evidence supports fault?

00:11:26.556 --> 00:11:29.087
And are there comparative negligence factors?

00:11:31.637 --> 00:11:36.033
The third piece is case projection: the early estimate of a case's value,

00:11:36.033 --> 00:11:37.779
risk, and outcome trajectory.

00:11:38.129 --> 00:11:41.971
It is based on coverage availability, liability strength,

00:11:41.971 --> 00:11:46.488
injury severity, treatment expectations, and documentation quality.

00:11:46.838 --> 00:11:51.325
These three pieces work together, and you cannot project a case accurately without

00:11:51.325 --> 00:11:51.981
all of them.

00:11:52.331 --> 00:11:54.990
Coverage asks: is there money to pay the claim?

00:11:55.340 --> 00:11:58.057
Liability asks: who is responsible?

00:11:58.407 --> 00:12:03.016
And projection asks: what is this case likely worth, and how will it move?

00:12:03.366 --> 00:12:06.175
Remember that the case's value is the attorney's call.

00:12:06.525 --> 00:12:10.113
Your projection is support for that decision, not a promise to the client.

00:12:12.663 --> 00:12:16.157
Let's apply this to our case, John Doe versus Apex.

00:12:16.507 --> 00:12:19.189
The snapshot: our client is John Doe.

00:12:19.539 --> 00:12:22.477
The defendant is Apex, a commercial trucking company.

00:12:22.827 --> 00:12:27.269
The incident is a rear-end motor vehicle collision involving a commercial vehicle,

00:12:27.269 --> 00:12:31.604
during a highway traffic slowdown, and the date of loss is pending verification.

00:12:31.954 --> 00:12:33.602
Now the coverage sources.

00:12:33.952 --> 00:12:39.142
Apex's commercial auto liability is assumed active, and it is the primary source.

00:12:39.492 --> 00:12:42.801
The employer umbrella is unknown and must be verified.

00:12:43.151 --> 00:12:47.969
The client's PIP and MedPay require insurance details from the client.

00:12:48.319 --> 00:12:52.626
And UM/UIM coverage is pending, depending on the Apex policy.

00:12:52.976 --> 00:12:57.744
Because Apex is a commercial carrier, substantial liability coverage is likely,

00:12:57.744 --> 00:13:00.581
with limits higher than a standard auto policy.

00:13:00.931 --> 00:13:04.188
The insight: coverage looks strong on the defendant's side,

00:13:04.188 --> 00:13:07.722
but it must be confirmed before the projection can be finalized.

00:13:10.272 --> 00:13:11.457
Now liability.

00:13:11.807 --> 00:13:15.658
The fact pattern: a rear-end collision with a commercial vehicle,

00:13:15.658 --> 00:13:16.961
in a traffic slowdown.

00:13:17.311 --> 00:13:19.552
Our client was struck from behind.

00:13:19.902 --> 00:13:23.745
The defendant may have been following too closely, or failed to brake.

00:13:24.095 --> 00:13:26.069
And there is no police report yet.

00:13:26.419 --> 00:13:30.462
Rear-end collisions involving commercial vehicles generally create a strong

00:13:30.462 --> 00:13:31.864
presumption of negligence.

00:13:32.214 --> 00:13:37.163
The Apex driver may be liable for negligent operation and failure to keep a safe

00:13:37.163 --> 00:13:41.618
distance, and Apex itself may carry vicarious liability as the employer.

00:13:41.968 --> 00:13:45.393
But note the risk: liability is strong, not final.

00:13:45.743 --> 00:13:48.557
It still needs confirmation from the police report,

00:13:48.557 --> 00:13:51.536
the driver's statement, and dashcam or other evidence.

00:13:51.886 --> 00:13:56.600
So, liability is likely favorable, but still in the pre-confirmation stage.

00:13:59.150 --> 00:14:03.376
Before clinical planning begins, you must clear the Regulatory Triple-Check,

00:14:03.376 --> 00:14:07.102
so the claim is properly established and you are authorized to act.

00:14:07.452 --> 00:14:12.260
One, HIPAA compliance: obtain a claim-specific authorization to release medical

00:14:12.260 --> 00:14:12.990
information.

00:14:13.340 --> 00:14:16.837
A standard release may not be enough for a personal injury case;

00:14:16.837 --> 00:14:21.262
it should name the incident date and allow communication with legal and insurance

00:14:21.262 --> 00:14:21.700
parties.

00:14:22.050 --> 00:14:27.049
Two, Medicare and Medicaid reporting: if the client is 65+ or on SSDI,

00:14:27.049 --> 00:14:31.691
report the case to the Benefits Coordination and Recovery Center,

00:14:31.691 --> 00:14:37.119
the BCRC, to avoid severe federal penalties and possible settlement freezes.

00:14:37.469 --> 00:14:41.719
This duty comes from the MMSEA, the Medicare, Medicaid,

00:14:41.719 --> 00:14:44.191
and SCHIP Extension Act of 2007.

00:14:44.541 --> 00:14:47.908
Three, subrogation management: determine the payer order.

00:14:48.258 --> 00:14:52.791
Typically that is PIP or MedPay first, then private health insurance,

00:14:52.791 --> 00:14:55.747
then Letter of Protection and provider liens.

00:14:58.297 --> 00:15:01.868
Once the claim is set up, the case plan must be filed,

00:15:01.868 --> 00:15:05.042
often within 30–60 days, depending on the state.

00:15:05.392 --> 00:15:07.110
Structure it in three pillars.

00:15:07.460 --> 00:15:12.023
Clinical: the acute interventions, such as physical therapy and specialty consults.

00:15:12.373 --> 00:15:16.889
Functional: activities of daily living, and the client's return to work status.

00:15:17.239 --> 00:15:21.923
And subrogation management, which is the payer order we just covered:

00:15:21.923 --> 00:15:26.945
PIP or MedPay, then private health insurance, then LOP and provider liens.

00:15:29.495 --> 00:15:32.523
US Case Managers have a duty to keep a clean file,

00:15:32.523 --> 00:15:35.370
because the file is discoverable in litigation.

00:15:35.720 --> 00:15:36.997
Three things to track.

00:15:37.347 --> 00:15:42.723
Electronic record integrity: make sure your case management system is SOC2 compliant

00:15:42.723 --> 00:15:45.091
and keeps an unalterable audit trail.

00:15:45.441 --> 00:15:49.417
State-specific timelines: in no-fault states like New York or Florida,

00:15:49.417 --> 00:15:53.677
the framework must include a timeline for independent medical examinations,

00:15:53.677 --> 00:15:56.575
which the carrier will require early in the set-up.

00:15:56.925 --> 00:16:00.587
And Letter of Protection management: if the client is uninsured,

00:16:00.587 --> 00:16:03.791
the plan must track every provider working under an LOP,

00:16:03.791 --> 00:16:07.339
to avoid surprise medical bills that exceed the policy limits.

00:16:09.889 --> 00:16:14.080
Here is the golden rule of claim set-up: coordinate, don't commingle.

00:16:14.430 --> 00:16:18.213
Treat the medical plan and the legal claim as two parallel tracks that never

00:16:18.213 --> 00:16:19.109
cross-contaminate.

00:16:19.459 --> 00:16:22.071
Never offer legal opinions in medical notes.

00:16:22.421 --> 00:16:27.018
Defense counsel often uses those notes to attack the Case Manager's neutrality.

00:16:27.368 --> 00:16:30.793
Keep your file clean, because everything in it can be discovered.

00:16:33.343 --> 00:16:35.294
Now we reach the Treatment Phase.

00:16:35.644 --> 00:16:40.891
Here your focus shifts from: what does the client need? to: is the plan working?

00:16:41.241 --> 00:16:43.215
Your role rests on three pillars.

00:16:43.565 --> 00:16:47.754
Facilitation: making sure the client can actually access services,

00:16:47.754 --> 00:16:51.181
which means transportation, paperwork, and scheduling.

00:16:51.531 --> 00:16:54.511
Coordination: making sure the therapist, the doctor,

00:16:54.511 --> 00:16:57.719
and the social worker are not working at cross-purposes.

00:16:58.069 --> 00:17:02.855
And advocacy: stepping in when a client is denied service or hits a bureaucratic

00:17:02.855 --> 00:17:03.154
wall.

00:17:03.504 --> 00:17:06.390
This phase is the engine room of case management,

00:17:06.390 --> 00:17:09.863
where set goals are achieved, and you must balance support,

00:17:09.863 --> 00:17:11.748
monitoring, and problem-solving.

00:17:14.297 --> 00:17:17.421
Your key responsibilities in treatment come in three parts.

00:17:17.771 --> 00:17:20.976
Monitoring progress: you are not just checking boxes;

00:17:20.976 --> 00:17:24.121
you are evaluating clinical and functional outcomes.

00:17:24.471 --> 00:17:27.883
At check-ins, ask specific questions about the plan,

00:17:27.883 --> 00:17:31.230
like whether the client attended therapy, not just:

00:17:31.230 --> 00:17:32.018
how are you?

00:17:32.368 --> 00:17:37.418
And document using GIRP or SOAP notes to track changes and milestones.

00:17:37.768 --> 00:17:42.818
Identifying barriers: treatment rarely goes perfectly, so be a barrier detective.

00:17:43.168 --> 00:17:47.835
Internal barriers include motivation, relapse, and mental health symptoms.

00:17:48.185 --> 00:17:53.596
External barriers include housing loss, childcare shortages, and insurance lapses.

00:17:53.946 --> 00:17:56.986
And crisis intervention: if a client faces a crisis,

00:17:56.986 --> 00:18:01.721
such as an eviction or a medical emergency, pause or adjust the treatment plan to

00:18:01.721 --> 00:18:03.826
deal with the immediate issue first.

00:18:06.376 --> 00:18:09.510
Three communication strategies keep clients on track.

00:18:09.860 --> 00:18:14.207
Motivational interviewing helps clients stay committed once the newness of treatment

00:18:14.207 --> 00:18:14.725
wears off.

00:18:15.075 --> 00:18:19.394
Boundaries prevent burnout, and keep the focus on the client's self-sufficiency.

00:18:19.744 --> 00:18:22.854
And active listening makes sure the client feels heard,

00:18:22.854 --> 00:18:25.398
and reduces misunderstandings with providers.

00:18:25.748 --> 00:18:28.395
Remember, the Treatment Phase is not linear.

00:18:28.745 --> 00:18:29.709
It is a cycle.

00:18:30.059 --> 00:18:34.834
Regularly assess the client's response, evaluate how well the plan is working,

00:18:34.834 --> 00:18:36.548
and adjust the intervention.

00:18:36.898 --> 00:18:41.134
If a client isn't reaching their goals, the cause is usually the plan,

00:18:41.134 --> 00:18:42.042
not the person.

00:18:42.392 --> 00:18:46.943
Modifying the plan is proactive management, not a mistake in your first evaluation.

00:18:49.493 --> 00:18:53.057
Your notes are the legal and professional record of treatment success.

00:18:53.407 --> 00:18:55.857
Structure them with the GIRP format.

00:18:56.207 --> 00:18:59.562
Goal: what part of the treatment plan are we addressing today?

00:18:59.912 --> 00:19:01.932
Intervention: what did you do?

00:19:02.282 --> 00:19:04.708
Response: how did the client react?

00:19:05.058 --> 00:19:08.414
And Plan: what is the next step before the next meeting?

00:19:08.764 --> 00:19:11.925
At the midpoint of treatment, many clients plateau,

00:19:11.925 --> 00:19:15.335
as the early excitement fades and the hard work begins.

00:19:15.685 --> 00:19:18.216
That is where motivational interviewing helps.

00:19:18.566 --> 00:19:23.210
Develop discrepancy: help the client see the gap between their current behavior and

00:19:23.210 --> 00:19:24.441
their long-term goals.

00:19:24.791 --> 00:19:28.982
Roll with resistance: if a client refuses a service, don't argue.

00:19:29.332 --> 00:19:31.131
Pivot, and explore why.

00:19:31.481 --> 00:19:34.620
And use OARS: open-ended questions, affirmations,

00:19:34.620 --> 00:19:36.926
reflective listening, and summaries.

00:19:39.476 --> 00:19:43.064
You are the hub of the wheel, and the key skill here is the warm handoff.

00:19:43.414 --> 00:19:47.516
Instead of handing a client a phone number, a high-skill Case Manager says:

00:19:47.516 --> 00:19:52.057
I know someone at that office; let's call them together right now and schedule your

00:19:52.057 --> 00:19:52.713
appointment.

00:19:53.063 --> 00:19:57.299
That reduces the referral leak, where clients drop out of treatment during

00:19:57.299 --> 00:19:57.986
transitions.

00:19:58.336 --> 00:20:00.507
Next, manage boundaries.

00:20:00.857 --> 00:20:03.946
One of the biggest risks in this phase is mission creep:

00:20:03.946 --> 00:20:08.525
trying to solve every new problem, which dilutes the focus on the primary treatment

00:20:08.525 --> 00:20:08.856
goals.

00:20:09.206 --> 00:20:14.222
Prioritize, by asking whether a new issue must be solved to reach the primary goal.

00:20:14.572 --> 00:20:19.123
And empower: coach clients through problems instead of solving them yourself.

00:20:19.473 --> 00:20:22.700
The goal is to eventually make the Case Manager unnecessary.

00:20:23.050 --> 00:20:25.808
Pro tip: end every treatment-phase contact by asking:

00:20:25.808 --> 00:20:29.970
what is the one thing that might get in the way of reaching your goal this week?

00:20:32.520 --> 00:20:36.665
You need to know the standard US treatment protocols, and their red flags.

00:20:37.015 --> 00:20:41.458
Physical therapy and conservative care are often the baseline of treatment in a

00:20:41.458 --> 00:20:42.076
legal case.

00:20:42.426 --> 00:20:46.734
Red flags here signal a stagnating case, or over-treatment for billing.

00:20:47.084 --> 00:20:51.535
Watch for more than 12 sessions with no improvement in range of motion or pain,

00:20:51.535 --> 00:20:54.015
with no referral to a specialist or imaging.

00:20:54.365 --> 00:20:57.984
Watch for passive sessions, like heat packs, TENS units,

00:20:57.984 --> 00:21:03.154
or massage, with no active exercise, which adjusters see as feel-good treatment.

00:21:03.504 --> 00:21:07.747
And watch for two or more missed appointments, which lets the defense argue the

00:21:07.747 --> 00:21:08.821
injury isn't severe.

00:21:09.171 --> 00:21:14.153
Surgery is the most valuable part of a claim, and its red flags involve safety and

00:21:14.153 --> 00:21:18.467
the standard of care: surgery recommended before therapy or injections,

00:21:18.467 --> 00:21:22.173
outside an emergency; left and right switched in the records;

00:21:22.173 --> 00:21:25.150
a client told to just wait on a fever or redness;

00:21:25.150 --> 00:21:30.011
and sharp or mechanical pain after surgery, which may signal a hardware problem.

00:21:32.561 --> 00:21:36.137
Pain management and medication is the most scrutinized area,

00:21:36.137 --> 00:21:39.654
because of opioid regulations and medical necessity audits.

00:21:40.004 --> 00:21:44.709
Red flags: an opioid, a muscle relaxant, and a sedative prescribed together,

00:21:44.709 --> 00:21:49.660
which is a major safety risk and a liability if the client has an accident while

00:21:49.660 --> 00:21:50.279
medicated.

00:21:50.629 --> 00:21:55.090
A urine drug screen showing the client is not taking their prescribed medication,

00:21:55.090 --> 00:21:56.852
or is taking illicit substances.

00:21:57.202 --> 00:21:59.869
And dosages rising with no change in diagnosis,

00:21:59.869 --> 00:22:03.727
which suggests the treatment is failing, or an addiction is forming.

00:22:04.077 --> 00:22:08.396
Clients with traumatic brain injury are high-value cases that need great discretion.

00:22:08.746 --> 00:22:11.808
Red flags: memory loss never mentioned at the ER,

00:22:11.808 --> 00:22:16.308
but significant TBI symptoms three months later when the lawsuit starts,

00:22:16.308 --> 00:22:19.183
which needs immediate neurological validation.

00:22:19.533 --> 00:22:23.773
And behavioral health notes about general life stress rather than the trauma of the

00:22:23.773 --> 00:22:26.685
accident, which are rarely covered and look unreasonable.

00:22:29.235 --> 00:22:33.380
Before you can build a treatment plan, you need to understand the whole person,

00:22:33.380 --> 00:22:35.899
which means more than reviewing medical records.

00:22:36.249 --> 00:22:37.758
Assess four areas.

00:22:38.108 --> 00:22:41.295
Clinical evaluation: the client's physical, cognitive,

00:22:41.295 --> 00:22:43.066
and psychological impairments.

00:22:43.416 --> 00:22:47.955
Social determinants: housing, family support, and financial stability.

00:22:48.305 --> 00:22:51.784
Pre-morbid status: the client's baseline before the accident,

00:22:51.784 --> 00:22:56.061
so you can separate pre-existing conditions from accident-related injuries.

00:22:56.411 --> 00:23:00.677
And vocational and educational outlook: can the client return to their previous

00:23:00.677 --> 00:23:02.459
role, or do they need retraining?

00:23:05.009 --> 00:23:08.990
In a personal injury case, goals must be defensible and measurable,

00:23:08.990 --> 00:23:11.070
for clinicians and adjusters alike.

00:23:11.420 --> 00:23:12.685
Use SMART goals.

00:23:13.035 --> 00:23:17.760
Specific: not improve mobility, but walk 50 meters with a rolling walker.

00:23:18.110 --> 00:23:22.336
Measurable: use standardized scales, such as FIM scores,

00:23:22.336 --> 00:23:24.449
or the PHQ-9 for depression.

00:23:24.799 --> 00:23:28.178
Achievable: the goal is realistic for the prognosis.

00:23:28.528 --> 00:23:32.115
Relevant: it relates directly to the injuries in the claim.

00:23:32.465 --> 00:23:36.819
And time-bound: it has clear milestones, like by the 12-week review.

00:23:39.369 --> 00:23:43.734
The care coordination strategy maps out the specific interventions the client needs.

00:23:44.084 --> 00:23:47.242
A standard framework groups them into four categories.

00:23:47.592 --> 00:23:52.770
Acute and restorative care: surgery, physiotherapy, and occupational therapy.

00:23:53.120 --> 00:23:58.571
Psychological support: counseling or cognitive behavioral therapy for PTSD,

00:23:58.571 --> 00:24:00.969
anxiety, or adjustment disorders.

00:24:01.319 --> 00:24:06.764
Durable medical equipment: sourcing wheelchairs, ramps, or assistive technology.

00:24:07.114 --> 00:24:11.475
And rehabilitation milestones: moving from inpatient to outpatient,

00:24:11.475 --> 00:24:12.907
or to home-based care.

00:24:15.457 --> 00:24:20.039
This is the Standard Treatment Map: the typical path of care in a personal injury

00:24:20.039 --> 00:24:20.322
case.

00:24:20.672 --> 00:24:25.126
It begins with emergency medical services and emergency room treatment,

00:24:25.126 --> 00:24:26.569
then MRI and radiology.

00:24:26.919 --> 00:24:30.602
From there, care continues through providers such as EMC,

00:24:30.602 --> 00:24:34.026
chiropractic care, physical therapy, and orthopedics,

00:24:34.026 --> 00:24:35.383
then pain management.

00:24:35.733 --> 00:24:39.406
Where needed, the path moves to surgery, post-operative care,

00:24:39.406 --> 00:24:41.213
and long-term pain management.

00:24:41.563 --> 00:24:42.852
Learn this map well.

00:24:43.202 --> 00:24:47.776
In the next slides, you will use it to spot when a client drifts off course.

00:24:50.326 --> 00:24:54.578
Mastering the treatment map is the difference between a Case Manager who observes a

00:24:54.578 --> 00:24:56.166
recovery and one who drives it.

00:24:56.516 --> 00:25:00.953
Deviations from the map are the main reason for insurance denials and independent

00:25:00.953 --> 00:25:02.158
medical exam triggers.

00:25:02.508 --> 00:25:04.180
The map has four phases.

00:25:04.530 --> 00:25:11.322
Acute, days 1–14: the ER, imaging, bracing, rest and ice, and initial therapy.

00:25:11.672 --> 00:25:14.934
Your focus is establishing causation and MedPay coverage.

00:25:15.284 --> 00:25:19.765
Sub-acute, weeks 2–8: active physical therapy two to three times a week,

00:25:19.765 --> 00:25:21.321
and specialist follow-up.

00:25:21.671 --> 00:25:26.211
Your focus is monitoring functional gains, like range of motion and weight-bearing.

00:25:26.561 --> 00:25:32.110
Restorative, months 2–6: work hardening, injections, and psychological adjustment.

00:25:32.460 --> 00:25:35.607
Your focus is identifying barriers to returning to work.

00:25:35.957 --> 00:25:41.054
And maintenance, six months and beyond: a home exercise program and as-needed

00:25:41.054 --> 00:25:41.518
visits.

00:25:41.868 --> 00:25:45.978
Your focus is determining maximum medical improvement, or MMI.

00:25:48.528 --> 00:25:51.786
When a client drifts off the map, you must diagnose why,

00:25:51.786 --> 00:25:53.648
before the insurer ends funding.

00:25:53.998 --> 00:25:55.820
There are three kinds of variance.

00:25:56.170 --> 00:26:00.920
Clinical variance: secondary complications, such as a back injury that develops

00:26:00.920 --> 00:26:03.926
nerve pain that was not in the original ER report.

00:26:04.276 --> 00:26:08.395
Psychosocial variance: yellow flags like fear-avoidance behavior,

00:26:08.395 --> 00:26:11.184
depression, or no transportation to therapy.

00:26:11.534 --> 00:26:17.001
And systemic variance: delays in insurance authorization for an MRI or a specialist

00:26:17.001 --> 00:26:17.594
referral.

00:26:20.144 --> 00:26:24.306
You are the central router for information, and a breakdown in any of these three

00:26:24.306 --> 00:26:26.054
loops can stall a case for months.

00:26:26.403 --> 00:26:30.579
The clinical loop: make sure the physical therapist's notes line up with the

00:26:30.579 --> 00:26:31.732
orthopedist's orders.

00:26:32.082 --> 00:26:36.941
If therapy says ninety percent recovered and the orthopedist says total disability,

00:26:36.941 --> 00:26:39.048
the file has a documentation crisis.

00:26:39.398 --> 00:26:43.885
The legal loop, with the attorney: provide status reports that translate medical

00:26:43.885 --> 00:26:45.679
jargon into functional evidence.

00:26:46.029 --> 00:26:50.682
The attorney needs to know what the client can't do in daily life to calculate pain

00:26:50.682 --> 00:26:51.916
and suffering damages.

00:26:52.266 --> 00:26:56.377
And the financial loop, with the adjuster: send proactive updates before the

00:26:56.377 --> 00:27:00.867
six-week physician referral expires, to prevent gaps in treatment the defense could

00:27:00.867 --> 00:27:02.273
later call non-compliance.

00:27:04.823 --> 00:27:10.295
Under US law, in utilization review, every item in your monitoring framework must

00:27:10.295 --> 00:27:11.511
meet two criteria.

00:27:11.861 --> 00:27:15.646
Reasonable: is the treatment common for this mechanism of injury?

00:27:15.996 --> 00:27:20.300
And necessary: is there objective evidence, not just subjective pain,

00:27:20.300 --> 00:27:22.857
that the treatment is improving function?

00:27:23.207 --> 00:27:24.948
Then apply the red flag rule.

00:27:25.298 --> 00:27:30.051
If a client's Oswestry Disability Index score has not changed after 12 weeks of

00:27:30.051 --> 00:27:32.578
physical therapy, the map shows a plateau.

00:27:32.928 --> 00:27:36.326
A proactive Case Manager seeks a specialist or a second opinion,

00:27:36.326 --> 00:27:39.035
instead of continuing treatment that isn't working.

00:27:41.585 --> 00:27:44.894
When you spot a red flag, follow the A-C-T protocol.

00:27:45.243 --> 00:27:49.564
Assess: is this a medical danger, like a fever or hardware failure,

00:27:49.564 --> 00:27:53.045
or a legal danger, like gaps in care or generic notes?

00:27:53.395 --> 00:27:57.320
Clarify: call the provider's medical assistant and ask directly.

00:27:57.670 --> 00:28:01.790
For example: the client has had 15 therapy sessions with no progress;

00:28:01.790 --> 00:28:04.717
is the doctor considering an orthopedic referral?

00:28:05.067 --> 00:28:08.898
And Track: document the red flag and your attempt to fix it.

00:28:09.248 --> 00:28:13.297
If the attorney ever needs to drop a provider who is damaging the case,

00:28:13.297 --> 00:28:15.750
your documentation provides the reason why.

00:28:18.300 --> 00:28:21.550
This flowchart puts the whole treatment map on one page.

00:28:21.900 --> 00:28:25.432
Phase one, acute, days 1 to 14: initial set-up,

00:28:25.432 --> 00:28:31.369
with ER or urgent care diagnostics, establishing causation and the mechanism of

00:28:31.369 --> 00:28:34.300
injury, and PIP or MedPay verification.

00:28:34.650 --> 00:28:40.077
Phase two, sub-acute, weeks 2 to 8: conservative care like therapy or chiropractic,

00:28:40.077 --> 00:28:41.581
and diagnostic imaging.

00:28:41.931 --> 00:28:44.857
Then the key question: is the patient improving?

00:28:45.207 --> 00:28:47.761
If yes, continue the treatment plan.

00:28:48.111 --> 00:28:51.917
If no, a variance is detected, and the variance loop begins:

00:28:51.917 --> 00:28:55.659
medication management or injections, a specialist referral,

00:28:55.659 --> 00:28:58.513
and psychological screening for yellow flags.

00:28:58.863 --> 00:29:03.314
Phase three, restorative: work hardening, a functional capacity evaluation,

00:29:03.314 --> 00:29:05.806
and the question of surgical intervention.

00:29:06.156 --> 00:29:10.612
And phase four, MMI and discharge: the permanent impairment rating,

00:29:10.612 --> 00:29:14.004
final lien resolution, and case settlement support.

00:29:16.554 --> 00:29:19.155
Here is how to use the map for course correction.

00:29:19.505 --> 00:29:22.907
First, identify the plateau, using the six-week rule.

00:29:23.257 --> 00:29:26.786
Most adjusters look for a functional gap at the six-week mark.

00:29:27.136 --> 00:29:32.953
The map expects a 20–30% improvement in range of motion, or a drop in pain scores.

00:29:33.303 --> 00:29:38.585
If the client is still at 10/10 pain after 12 therapy sessions, pivot immediately.

00:29:38.935 --> 00:29:42.151
Don't wait for the adjuster to deny the next six sessions.

00:29:42.501 --> 00:29:47.261
Second, monitor with objective metrics, to keep the loop with the insurer tight.

00:29:47.611 --> 00:29:52.365
Use the Oswestry or DASH score to measure disability in daily activities,

00:29:52.365 --> 00:29:53.277
every 30 days.

00:29:53.627 --> 00:29:58.178
Use gait analysis for mobility and fall risk in lower-extremity injuries.

00:29:58.528 --> 00:30:04.406
Use the PHQ-9 and GAD-7 to screen for depression and anxiety when physical progress

00:30:04.406 --> 00:30:04.902
stalls.

00:30:05.252 --> 00:30:10.174
And use a functional capacity evaluation before a return to work, or at MMI.

00:30:12.724 --> 00:30:15.487
Watch for psychosocial barriers that derail the map.

00:30:15.837 --> 00:30:20.261
Catastrophizing: I will never walk again, despite minor clinical findings.

00:30:20.611 --> 00:30:24.953
Litigation stress: high conflict with the defense side slowing recovery.

00:30:25.303 --> 00:30:30.247
And iatrogenic factors: dependency on opioids, or too much passive care,

00:30:30.247 --> 00:30:34.092
like ice and heat, instead of active care like exercise.

00:30:34.442 --> 00:30:37.279
When you communicate with the attorney and the insurer,

00:30:37.279 --> 00:30:38.981
use the map to justify your plan.

00:30:39.331 --> 00:30:42.559
For example: the client is in phase two, sub-acute.

00:30:42.909 --> 00:30:47.523
We have noted a clinical variance: physical therapy has not reduced the radicular

00:30:47.523 --> 00:30:47.808
pain.

00:30:48.158 --> 00:30:52.838
Per the Standard Treatment Map, I am recommending an MRI and a neurosurgical consult

00:30:52.838 --> 00:30:55.623
to rule out surgical necessity before phase three.

00:30:55.973 --> 00:30:58.516
This keeps treatment reasonable and necessary.

00:31:01.066 --> 00:31:05.862
Treatment gaps found during active care can weaken the credibility of the injury,

00:31:05.862 --> 00:31:09.001
delay recovery, create documentation inconsistencies,

00:31:09.001 --> 00:31:11.074
and reduce future settlement value.

00:31:11.424 --> 00:31:14.848
Intervening early prevents bigger problems in the demand phase.

00:31:15.198 --> 00:31:19.460
The common causes are: missed appointments, transportation problems,

00:31:19.460 --> 00:31:22.907
financial concerns or co-pays, work schedule conflicts,

00:31:22.907 --> 00:31:26.104
a client stopping treatment after temporary relief,

00:31:26.104 --> 00:31:29.363
and delays in referrals, imaging, or authorizations.

00:31:31.913 --> 00:31:36.005
Your responsibilities with treatment gaps: monitor treatment frequency and

00:31:36.005 --> 00:31:37.276
appointment compliance.

00:31:37.626 --> 00:31:40.227
Follow up immediately after a missed appointment.

00:31:40.577 --> 00:31:43.712
Educate the client on why consistent treatment matters.

00:31:44.062 --> 00:31:47.568
Help coordinate transportation, scheduling, or referrals.

00:31:47.918 --> 00:31:50.913
Communicate with providers about interruptions in care.

00:31:51.263 --> 00:31:55.013
And document every client explanation and every follow-up effort.

00:31:55.363 --> 00:31:58.524
As best practice, keep treatment timelines updated,

00:31:58.524 --> 00:32:03.419
track referrals and pending appointments, encourage clients to report worsening

00:32:03.419 --> 00:32:08.501
symptoms promptly, and escalate prolonged or unexplained gaps to supervising staff

00:32:08.501 --> 00:32:09.493
or the attorney.

00:32:09.842 --> 00:32:14.026
Consistent treatment documentation strengthens both the medical support and the

00:32:14.026 --> 00:32:15.508
future negotiating position.

00:32:18.058 --> 00:32:21.286
When a bottleneck occurs, use the three-step escalation.

00:32:21.636 --> 00:32:26.477
Level one, clinical: contact the provider's office manager to expedite notes or a

00:32:26.477 --> 00:32:27.673
peer-to-peer review.

00:32:28.023 --> 00:32:32.260
Level two, administrative: send a notice of delay to the adjuster and the

00:32:32.260 --> 00:32:35.395
plaintiff's attorney, documenting the barrier to care.

00:32:35.745 --> 00:32:39.791
Level three, legal: ask the attorney to file a motion to compel,

00:32:39.791 --> 00:32:43.837
or to use a Letter of Protection to get past insurance stalling.

00:32:44.187 --> 00:32:46.927
That last decision always belongs to the attorney.

00:32:49.477 --> 00:32:53.216
When the treatment map hits a wall, move up the escalation ladder.

00:32:53.566 --> 00:32:58.289
Step A, the clinician: request a peer-to-peer review between the treating doctor and

00:32:58.289 --> 00:33:00.032
the insurer's medical director.

00:33:00.382 --> 00:33:05.263
Step B, the adjuster: send a status update that states plainly that the delay is

00:33:05.263 --> 00:33:10.204
increasing the total claim cost, by extending the period of temporary disability.

00:33:10.554 --> 00:33:14.110
Step C, the attorney: if step B fails, notify the attorney,

00:33:14.110 --> 00:33:19.111
who may issue a 30-day demand, or move the provider to a Letter of Protection basis

00:33:19.111 --> 00:33:21.642
to avoid the insurance authorization wait.

00:33:24.192 --> 00:33:27.465
Skill-building: John Doe versus Apex Delivery Services,

00:33:27.465 --> 00:33:29.846
with Aggressive Casualty as the carrier.

00:33:30.196 --> 00:33:34.992
Intake has surfaced a critical complication: the eggshell plaintiff narrative,

00:33:34.992 --> 00:33:37.452
against a prior lumbar strain from 2018.

00:33:37.802 --> 00:33:40.658
The carrier has seen the 2018 record.

00:33:41.008 --> 00:33:44.910
It is stalling the MRI for the L4-L5 protrusion in your intake,

00:33:44.910 --> 00:33:48.193
claiming it is a pre-existing degenerative condition,

00:33:48.193 --> 00:33:51.167
not caused by the collision with the Apex truck.

00:33:51.517 --> 00:33:54.268
Your objective is to support proving aggravation.

00:33:54.618 --> 00:33:59.434
Under US law, an eggshell plaintiff is entitled to full recovery for the increase in

00:33:59.434 --> 00:34:03.906
disability, even if they were more vulnerable to injury than a healthy person.

00:34:04.256 --> 00:34:08.060
Think about how you would document the client's baseline and the change since the

00:34:08.060 --> 00:34:10.595
accident, and what you would escalate to the attorney.

00:34:13.145 --> 00:34:17.569
Our final exercise is a flash simulation: the transportation wall.

00:34:17.919 --> 00:34:20.969
John is in a 12-week intensive chiropractic program,

00:34:20.969 --> 00:34:23.491
and he has been doing great for four weeks.

00:34:23.841 --> 00:34:24.851
Then he calls you.

00:34:25.201 --> 00:34:27.326
His car's transmission just died.

00:34:27.676 --> 00:34:30.370
He says: I can't get to the clinic anymore.

00:34:30.720 --> 00:34:32.426
It's a 45-minute drive.

00:34:32.776 --> 00:34:36.921
I might as well quit the program and try again next year when I have money saved.

00:34:37.271 --> 00:34:41.404
Your task: decide how you will handle this common treatment-phase obstacle.

00:34:41.754 --> 00:34:46.259
Think about treatment gaps, warm handoffs, and the barriers we discussed today.

00:34:46.609 --> 00:34:51.148
Then create a case file in the training interface, and update it with your plan.

00:34:53.698 --> 00:34:55.138
That completes Day 1.

00:34:55.488 --> 00:34:59.720
Today you learned what a Case Manager owns, how to run a clean intake and acceptance

00:34:59.720 --> 00:35:03.650
decision, how to set up a claim, and how to keep the treatment phase on track.

00:35:04.000 --> 00:35:05.300
Thank you for your attention.

00:35:05.650 --> 00:35:07.682
Bring your questions to the next session.

